Stop a forked child from resuming greenlets that were blocked at the fork.

The companion to cancelling the queued switches. That queue only holds greenlets
that were *runnable*; one that was blocked is parked on a watcher, and the loop
resumes it from there without consulting the queue. Both are copies in the
child, and neither may run: on an inherited TLS session such a copy writes a
record the parent will write again under the same sequence number, drawing
``bad_record_mac`` from the server, and on an inherited pipe it consumes bytes
the parent was capturing.

What identifies such a copy is the watcher's callback: gevent sets it when a
greenlet starts waiting and clears it when the wait ends, so a watcher whose
callback is a ``Waiter.switch`` is one a greenlet is blocked on. The watcher's
``active`` and ``pending`` flags cannot answer this. Under libuv an expired timer
has already been stopped by the time its callback is dispatched, and ``pending``
there is a constant ``False``, so a greenlet whose ``sleep`` has just come due
looks idle in the very window a fork is most likely to land in.

Only waits are cancelled, and only other greenlets'. Anything else is left alone,
including signal handlers, which a child inherits by ordinary POSIX rules, and
:class:`gevent.Timeout`, whose timer may belong to a ``with`` block the forking
greenlet is still inside.

The cancellation also stops at the wait. A child of a plain ``os.fork()`` may
legitimately carry on using the objects it inherited, as :mod:`gevent.os`
documents and as gevent's own bind/fork/accept server pattern requires, so
whatever the wait was on has to come out of this fit to use. Under libuv that
rules out stopping the watcher for a socket at all: one ``epoll`` registration
serves every wait on a given descriptor, and it lives in a descriptor the child
inherited rather than in memory it copied, so a child that deregistered its own
wait would silence the parent's socket too, and the parent would wait forever for
a connection it never hears about. There the callback alone is cleared, which is
what the loop checks before dispatching. (libev batches the same call until its
next loop iteration, which a child that ``exec``s never reaches, and ``reinit``
gives a child that keeps running an ``epoll`` set of its own.)

Finding those watchers costs one ``gc.get_objects()`` walk per fork in the
child, which measured about 2ms against a 690,000 object heap, against 70ms for
the spawn it accompanies.
